      <outline text="Conflicts (0 open)">
        <outline text="HOT-001 — Does `Trucking` mean third-party freight (no wage labor) or an own long-haul run (labor-bearing)? T…" _note="Decides whether a trucked fulfillment absorbs labor cost at all, and therefore whether `trucked` is a fulfillment mode that generates a shift.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-010"/>
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0011"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-002 — Are the collecting/returning flags really per-leg? Only ~2% of revenue sits on asymmetric combinati…" _note="If the flags are genuinely per-destination rather than per-leg, then a leg record cannot be reconstructed from history, and the migration cannot backfill leg-level actor or shift.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-007"/>
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0011"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-003 — Does the `Crew` product line already carry labor today?" _note="If Crew already carries labor, the new cost-type dimension partly duplicates it and the migration has to decide which one survives.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-011"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-004 — Should long-haul absorb a byproduct-loss premium on top of the person-day, mirroring what the custo…" _note="A long-haul run consumes a crew-day that cannot pick up byproducts, so its true opportunity cost exceeds its wage cost. Charging only the person-day understates long-haul COGS and makes long-haul look more profitable than it is.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-006"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-005 — Is TigerBeetle or DuckDB the reporting source of truth, given TB timestamps are posting time and re…" _note="Every report — trial balance, P&amp;L, close — is periodised by accounting date. If TB cannot express that, TB balances cannot answer a period question directly and DuckDB becomes authoritative for reporting while TB stays authoritative for balance integrity. That split has to be deliberate, named, and reconciled.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-009"/>
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0008"/>
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0010"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-006 — Xero history migration: import known-defective lines, or restate? Roughly 90% of historical Xero li…" _note="Importing defective lines makes the new ledger's history agree with the filed tax position but carries the tracking defect forward permanently. Restating makes the new ledger correct but disagrees with what was filed.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-012"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-007 — Are fulfillment legs first-class recorded events, or derived from orders? The current system derive…" _note="This is the single decision the whole labor-costing design rests on. Derived legs cannot carry an actor, a clock-in, or a shift id, so COGS labor allocation is impossible without first-class legs.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-001"/>
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0011"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-008 — Duplicate ACTIVE charge products book the same economic event to different revenue accounts, so the…" _note="The new posting rules must map a billed line to exactly one account. Today the account depends on which of two identically-named active products the operator happened to pick, so historical revenue is split across accounts for no economic reason — and any posting rule derived from history inherits the ambiguity.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-014"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-009 — `invoice.status` carries two different semantics in one field — an assigned lifecycle fact (draft /…" _note="ADR-0014 makes derived state a rebuildable projection and assigned state a recorded fact. A field that is both cannot be either, and the two live defect populations are one of each: the derived half going stale, and the lifecycle half disagreeing with the balance. Decomposing the field is a precondition for applying ADR-0014 to invoices at all.">
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0014"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-010 — The charter and `posting-rules.yaml` specify standard-cost absorption with a `labor_variance` posti…" _note="A rate variance cannot exist where there is no standard rate, so `labor_variance` as specified has no input and would be a posting rule that can never fire. Absorption itself survives and must not be discarded with it: a guaranteed 8 hours means paid-but-unworked time is a real cost attributable to no job, so `COGS-Labor Absorbed` and `COGS-Unabsorbed Labor` both stand. What they measure is UTILISATION, not rate deviation, and the posting rules must say which.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-018"/>
          <outline text="blocks OQ-019"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-011 — REQ-LED-001 and ADR-0018 require BOTH dimensions on every revenue and COGS posting; `ledger/dimensi…" _note="REQ-LED-001 is the repo's only ledger requirement and the golden vectors' rejection cases are its entire enforcement, so which reading is authoritative decides what those vectors assert. Written as REQ-LED-001 states it, the rule refuses legitimate postings — interest income has no product line and no &quot;kind of work&quot;. Written as `dimensions.yaml` states it, the requirement's own text is wrong. `ledger/chart-of-accounts.yaml`'s `dimensioned:` column cannot be set correctly until one of them wins, and 8 accounts currently carry `dimensioned: undecided` waiting on it.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-021"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-012 — `ADR-0001` is `accepted` and therefore immutable, and its Context states a number that has been ret…" _note="Not because the decision is in doubt — replacing Xero stands on the costing model Xero cannot express, on ADR-0026's dual-basis requirement, and on three other Context bullets that are unaffected. It matters because **the repo has no mechanism for an accepted ADR whose evidence was later corrected**, and this is the first instance. Every other artifact citing the 28.7% was amended in place on 2026-08-10; `adr/` is the one lifecycle where that is forbidden (&quot;immutable once accepted — superseded by a new ADR, never edited&quot;). So the correction has nowhere to live, and a reader of `ADR-0001` today gets a number that is wrong by a factor of ~700 and a causal claim that points at the wrong system. The general question is the one to settle: **is an ADR a historical record of a decision as taken, or a statement of current fact?** If the former, this hotspot resolves by convention — the correction lives in `inbox/` and the traceability matrix, and ADR-0001 is left alone as the honest record of what was believed on 2026-08-08. If the latter, an accepted ADR needs a lightweight errata mechanism, because superseding ADR-0001 to fix a Context bullet would mean re-deciding &quot;replace Xero&quot;, which nobody is asking to reopen."/>
        <outline text="HOT-013 — `ADR-0018` says dimensions are &quot;carried on the posting, in `user_data` and in the Mongo/Parquet pro…" _note="It decides whether the ledger is self-describing. If TigerBeetle carries the dimensions, a dimensional P&amp;L can be rebuilt from the ledger alone after a Mongo loss; if it does not, TigerBeetle holds balance integrity and the dimensional view exists only in the projection — which is defensible under ADR-0017, but is a different system from the one ADR-0018's sentence describes. The ambiguity is load-bearing rather than cosmetic, because REQ-LED-001's whole enforcement story rests on it. `ledger/dimensions.yaml` states that with dimensions off account identity &quot;nothing structural catches a missing one — the rejection vectors are the entire enforcement&quot;. That reasoning assumed the dimension is on the posting the vectors check. If dimensions live only in the projection, the vectors are checking a different artifact from the one the ledger stores, and REQ-LED-001 is being enforced somewhere other than where it is stated.">
          <outline text="blocks OQ-040"/>
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0033"/>
          <outline text="blocks SPIKE-003"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-014 — ADR-0029 requires that &quot;every posting must carry its causal order, or allocation is impossible and…" _note="ADR-0029 splits the ledger from the report on the promise that the un-allocated posting keeps enough to allocate downstream — &quot;an un-allocated posting that carries its causal order can be allocated downstream by any basis a question needs&quot;. The causal order is the half of that promise the ledger does not keep. ADR-0031's official product-line P&amp;L allocates `goods_revenue_on_causal_order` with `scope: causal_order`, so the ONE official allocation is keyed on a value the accounting record does not hold. **The consequence is asymmetric and it is the wrong way round.** After a MongoDB loss, TigerBeetle rebuilds balances (ADR-0003), periods (`user_data_32`) and — under ADR-0035 — dimensions. What it cannot rebuild is the allocation, because the causal order is gone. So the recoverable view is exactly the one ADR-0029 says &quot;must never be read as a managed P&amp;L&quot;, and the unrecoverable one is what it calls &quot;the managed number&quot;. This is not the same gap as HOT-013. HOT-013 asked where a DIMENSION lives and is answered by ADR-0035. This asks where the ALLOCATION KEY lives, and ADR-0035 explicitly does not answer it.">
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0029"/>
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0031"/>
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0036"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-015 — `contexts/ledger/features/posting-keys.feature` — then named `dimensional-postings.feature` — still…" _note="This is **HOT-011 surviving in the one artifact its resolution did not sweep**. ADR-0025 amended REQ-LED-001, re-scoped `cost_type` in `ledger/dimensions.yaml` and set the per-account `dimensions:` lists in `ledger/chart-of-accounts.yaml` — three artifacts. The `.feature` file was left stating the refuted reading, and it is not a fourth copy of the rule: gate 3 ties every requirement to a Gherkin scenario, so **these scenarios are REQ-LED-001's only executable statement**. A scenario that overstates the obligation is what an implementer builds to, and building it re-introduces exactly what OQ-021 removed — a &quot;kind of work&quot; demanded on `5000 Cost of Goods Sold: Retail Inventory`, where none of `delivery` / `counter` / `warehouse` is true. It survived because **nothing reads a `.feature` file's dimension claims**. Gate 10 checks every golden vector in `ledger/vectors/` against `ledger/dimensions.yaml` and stops there (erp-spec#16). The value-set gate added for that issue does not catch this one either: every value used here — `Transport`, `delivery`, `Crew` — is legally DECLARED. What is wrong is APPLICABILITY, not vocabulary.">
          <outline text="blocks REQ-LED-001"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-016 — `adr/ADR-0030` — ACCEPTED and FROZEN 2026-08-16 — asserts as a supporting contrast that &quot;ADR-0019 c…" _note="⚠️ **The DECISION is unaffected and that is what makes this a hotspot rather than a supersession.** Vehicle absorption is still rate-based, 5901 still means utilisation plus rate deviation, and no account or basis in ADR-0030 changes. What is wrong is the CONTRAST it drew, not the conclusion it reached — ADR-0034's third row exactly: &quot;stands, but a fact it cited was wrong ⇒ no new ADR; a dated note in inbox/, plus a hotspots.yaml entry when it contradicts something&quot;. ⚠️ **FIRST USE OF THAT PATH.** HOT-006 through HOT-015 are all `resolved` and all carry `resolved_by: ADR-XXXX` — every one is &quot;two statements conflict and a DECISION settles it&quot;. None is &quot;an accepted ADR cited a fact that later measured false&quot;. Under this repo's own rule that an unexercised branch is a claim rather than a capability, the machinery was assumed not to work until taken. ⚠️ **And FIVE MUTABLE artifacts repeated the refuted claim, two of them left behind by the very amendment that found the defect.** ⚠️ **This entry said THREE until the sweep ran** — the count came from a single grep pattern, and it was wrong TWICE. Two surfaced under different phrasing on the day: the golden vector `guaranteed-hours-unworked-land-in-5801.yaml` (&quot;costing is actual at that person's own wage, so no standard rate exists to deviate from&quot;) and `glossary.yaml`'s `standard cost` term (&quot;the target system does not implement this&quot;). ⚠️ **And a SIXTH was found on 2026-08-17 while proposing a charter edit: `charter.md`'s in-scope bullet said &quot;**actual**-cost absorption into COGS, where the absorbed/unabsorbed split measures **utilisation** rather than rate variance&quot; — both halves refuted, in the repo's TOP-LEVEL document.** The sweep missed it because the pattern was &quot;costed at actual&quot; and the charter says &quot;actual-cost absorption&quot;. ⇒ **A count stated from one search is a count of that search** — recorded as a footgun in `CLAUDE.md` the same afternoon, and violated twice more before the day ended, including here. ADR-0019's body was corrected to the measurement while its TITLE and its SUMMARY BLOCKQUOTE were not — and ADR-0019 has **121 citations** across the spec, so a title is what most readers take away. `5800`'s chart reason repeated it too. ⚠️ **AND IT HAPPENED A FOURTH TIME, INSIDE THIS HOTSPOT.** The file was renamed `…-is-actual.md` → `…-is-normal-costing.md` as part of resolving this, and the evidence list above still cited the old path. **Gate 11 caught it** — which is the difference between this instance and the three before it: the earlier ones survived because nothing executed on them. **That is the HOT-011 / HOT-015 pattern for the third time** — HOT-015's own text calls itself &quot;HOT-011 surviving in the one artifact its resolution did not sweep&quot; — and the first time the incomplete sweep was made by the same session that opened the hotspot. **Amending a body is not amending a decision.**">
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0019"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-017 — `charter.md` states that a production service agreement's money moves through `2800 - PSA Liability…" _note="⚠️ **The charter asserts a MECHANISM, not a preference, and the mechanism decides the revenue figure.** Billing through a liability is the agency treatment: the client's budget is money CFS holds. Billing to 4100 is the principal treatment: it is CFS revenue. Net income is the same either way and **the top line and every margin computed from it are not** — so the two readings produce different financial statements from the same events. ⚠️ **Both treatments are live for the SAME CUSTOMER.** DePaul's four labor-contract invoices total $38,150 on 4100; its September set-dec invoice bills $7,000 through 2800. Nothing in the data distinguishes them, so the discriminator is whoever wrote the invoice. ⚠️ **And the same line NAME is coded to two accounts** — `Contract Labor` is 4120 for N2ition LLC and 4100 for 637 Films. **This is a spec statement against a measurement, which is HOT-016's shape** rather than two spec statements against each other. The charter is not wrong about what SHOULD happen; it asserts as settled a thing that has never been decided, and erp-spec#35 records that PSA has no requirement, no event and no posting rule to make it true. ⚠️ **`4130`'s $13,202.34 must not be used to size PSA** until it reconciles to something. It is 13% of the invoiced total and sits on an account no invoice line names, so whatever produced it is a path this repo has not seen."/>
        <outline text="HOT-018 — `adr/ADR-0019` — ACCEPTED and FROZEN 2026-08-17 — states that the capped components of employer bur…" _note="⚠️ **The DECISION is unaffected, which is what makes this a hotspot rather than a supersession** (ADR-0034 row 3, the same path HOT-016 took). Burden is still capped per person per year, a flat factor is still systematically wrong rather than imprecise, `labor_variance` still fires and the apportionment still owes a period-close true-up. ⚠️ **What is wrong is the SIZE, and it is wrong by about half.** The seasonal bias between early-year and late-year hours is 7.65 points of wage, not 13.85 — and sizing that bias is the number's entire job in ADR-0019. A true-up built to the stated figure would be scaled to roughly twice the real dispersion. ⚠️ **Every individual number in the sentence is correct and the sentence is internally consistent** — `1.45 + 3.06 + 1.49 = 6.00` and `19.85 − 6.00 = 13.85` both tie. That is why it survived acceptance, a six-reference survey, HOT-016's correction of the very same bullet, and twenty gates. **What it never states is which components the 13.85 is made of**, and the missing 6.20 is Social Security. ⇒ **It was found by attempting to TYPE the components** — writing them as a table with a cap-base column per row forces &quot;caps at what&quot; for every component, and Social Security is the row whose answer contradicts the prose. ⚠️ **AND THE OWNER'S RULING (2026-08-17) INVERTS THAT LESSON, which is the more useful half.** _&quot;we dont run payroll direct, we dont track futa or suta or any of that we use eors, pay subcontractors and loanouts (1099) why would we track that stuff?&quot;_ **CFS computes none of these components** — payroll processing for its own crew is a charter non-goal, and CFS's cost is the Wrapbook invoice. So the type that would have caught this is a type **the spec should not have**: carrying `BurdenComponent[]` would model a vendor's payroll engine as a side effect of wanting a rate. ⇒ **The root cause is not a missing column, it is reasoning two layers down into someone else's ledger.** What CFS can see, act on and verify is ONE number — the effective rate on a run, with its source document — plus the fact that it declines within a season. **A component breakdown of a number you do not compute is a claim you cannot check.**"/>
        <outline text="HOT-019 — `adr/ADR-0025` — `proposed` — decides that _&quot;Each entry in `ledger/chart-of-accounts.yaml` carries…" _note="⚠️ **This is not a stale citation, it is a decision that cannot be enacted.** ADR-0025 is the instrument that was to resolve HOT-011 and answer OQ-021, OQ-022 and OQ-025 — all four of which are already resolved or answered — so the ADR is simultaneously the record of settled rulings and a mechanism the repo has since forbidden. ⚠️ **Of its four decision clauses, only one is both live and undecided.** The per-account `dimensions:` list is refused by the gate; `Other`'s deletion was enacted by OQ-022 rather than by this ADR; the REQ-LED-001 amendment is dead as stated because postings carry no dimension to declare; and the `product_line: null` vs `4800 - Other Income` distinction survives in substance while its phrasing (&quot;a dimensioned account&quot;) does not. ⇒ **Nothing detected this.** `validate.ts` mentions supersession 36 times — gate 6 for symmetry, gate 14 to freeze bodies, and erp-spec#18 so the acceptance fields cannot be forgotten — and **never asks whether a `proposed` ADR rests on a `superseded` one**. ADR-0036 was the FIRST supersession declared from a `proposed` ADR, so it exercised the declare-and- enact path and not the notify-the-dependents path, which still does not exist. Two other proposed ADRs name ADR-0018 the same way: ADR-0020, whose Y-statement premise (&quot;a new ledger where that dimension is not nullable&quot;) no longer holds, and ADR-0029, which is REINFORCED because ADR-0036 preserved the half it relies on. ⚠️ **The 2026-08-20 rule 8a survey found the rule is defensible but pointed at the wrong field.** The criterion running through all five ERPs is not &quot;was this classified&quot; but &quot;can the book still be tied out if this is missing&quot; — which endorses ADR-0036's derive-at-report-time for `product_line`, and endorses the declare-or-be-rejected obligation sitting on **`causal_orders`**, a key that must tie out. ⚠️ **CORRECTED 2026-08-22 — this hotspot and the survey both named the wrong owner.** Both said ADR-0029 &quot;already requires&quot; `causal_orders` on every posting. **ADR-0029's body does not contain the string.** The owners are **ADR-0036** (accepted 2026-08-16, absorbing HOT-014 — &quot;That requirement is now this ADR's decision&quot;) and **REQ-LED-001**, restated the same day. It executes: 24 `causal_orders` declarations in `ledger/posting-rules.yaml` with golden vectors on all three arms. ⇒ **There is nothing to &quot;move&quot; — the obligation is already in force, and what the survey delivers is CONFIRMATION of an existing rule rather than a proposal.** Full note: `inbox/2026-08-22-correction-the-causal-orders-obligation-is-adr-0036s-decision-not-adr-0029s-and-two-artifacts-carried-the-wrong-owner.md`.">
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0025"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-020 — `adr/ADR-0020` — `proposed` — faces _&quot;a new ledger where that dimension **is not nullable**&quot;_ and d…" _note="⚠️ **Accepting it would freeze the contradiction permanently.** A body freezes at acceptance (ADR-0034, gate 14), and ADR-0020 now cites a 2026-08-20 survey that concludes its own premise is void. **Citing evidence against yourself and then freezing is worse than either alone.** ⚠️ **The restatement is RELOCATED, not removed.** If the product line is derived at report time by joining line identity to the product master, what must be categorised is the **master** — which `api-cloudrun#473` already repaired on 2026-08-10, collapsing the population from 383 lines / $485,821.72 to **$688.00** of genuine operator non-classification. **The $688.00 is not the decision. The 128 custom lines with no product master are** — they have nothing to derive from, and `OQ-025` plus `reporting/product-line-pl.yaml` already hold the house pattern: a declared null, shown on its own row. ⚠️ **Two figures in the ADR and its neighbours are wrong, and one is load-bearing.** The &quot;~90% behind the 2025-12-31 lock&quot; figure, which appears undated in four live places, measures **80.04% of invoices** and ~**73.6–76.6% of lines** (`ADR-0020/M3`). And `charter.md`'s &quot;unrepairable&quot; is a **policy claim wearing a mechanical one** — a period lock is a setting, not a physical impossibility. Neither changes the direction of the decision; both change how it reads. ✅ **What the survey ADDS rather than removes**: the migration delta, previously called unmeasurable, is **$231,796.26 — 106 lines, 15.66% of pre-lock revenue, untracked in BOTH (`ADR-0020/M1`) systems**. And ADR-0020's own 13.90% is gone: lines where CFS holds a category and Xero does not are **7 / $1,700.00** (`ADR-0020/M4`), none since 2024-10-07 — so **Xero's tracking-sliced P&amp;L for closed periods is substantially complete**, and there IS a report to diverge from, which the ADR assumed away.">
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0020"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-021 — `adr/ADR-0029` — `proposed` — opens its Context with _&quot;**Allocation is destructive; grouping is not…" _note="⚠️ **Same grounds as HOT-019 and HOT-020, and opened for the same reason: accepting it would freeze the contradiction permanently.** A body freezes at acceptance (ADR-0034, gate 14), and ADR-0029 cites a survey whose opening finding is that ADR-0029's opening claim is false. **Citing evidence against yourself and then freezing is worse than either alone.** ⚠️ **The DECISION is not in doubt — the ARGUMENT for it is.** The survey found three of five systems post allocations, two have no engine, and **none derives at report time**, so ADR-0029 departs from every reference that has the feature. It survives on a better argument the ADR does not currently make: **a number whose correctness depends on a basis ADR-0031 itself calls the weakest defensible tier should not be given a document number.** That is SAP's own second criterion. ✅ And it has a production precedent nobody had cited — **Deltek Vision**, whose overhead allocation &quot;does not impact the general ledger… they are not posted to the database&quot;, with year-to-date recomputation giving it the same idempotence as `sealed_at_close: false`. ⭐ **The option ADR-0029 never considers is the one they all implement: nobody keeps allocations out of the LEDGER — they keep them out of the STATEMENTS.** SAP's `S Secondary Costs` account type, Intacct's mandatory allocation book, NetSuite's contra credit. Three implementations of one idea, and ADR-0029 has no Considered Options section at all. ⚠️ **And GAAP's hook is a BILL, not a free pass.** ASC 280-10-50-27 never mentions posting — the test is membership of the measure the CODM actually uses — but 50-29(b)/(d)/(e) demand the policy, quantified method changes and asymmetrical allocations, and **50-30 requires reconciliation to the consolidated totals.** ADR-0031's `basis_version` and `sealed_at_close: false` satisfy the first three by construction. **The reconciliation is stated nowhere in the reporting spec** — and it is the one that makes the un-allocated view load-bearing rather than merely available. ⚠️ ASC 280 exempts nonpublic entities, so this is a criterion for CFS rather than a requirement.">
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0029"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-022 — `adr/ADR-0015:61` — `proposed` — says _&quot;**The two-phase timeout is the compensation mechanism SPIKE…" _note="⭐ **PROVEN, not argued.** A third module — `expiring_timeout`, added 2026-08-22 — is the protocol AS SPECIFIED plus TigerBeetle's real `Transfer.timeout`. Its invariant fails under both randomised simulation and Apalache, and the counterexample is **three steps long with `dead: false` throughout**: `reserve → writeDoc → expire`. ⚠️ **NO CRASH IS REQUIRED.** This is not a crash-interleaving bug — it is a plain race between the writer and the clock. A writer merely slower than the timeout leaves a durable MongoDB document behind a transfer that TigerBeetle has already expired and that can never be posted. ⚠️ **The result code is real and pinned**: `pending_transfer_expired = 35` in `tigerbeetle-node@0.17.9`'s own `bindings.d.ts`, alongside `pending_transfer_already_posted` and `pending_transfer_already_voided`. Upstream is explicit: _&quot;If the timeout interval passes before the transfer is either posted or voided, the transfer expires and the full amount is returned to the original account.&quot;_ **TigerBeetle does it itself and cannot be asked not to once `timeout &gt; 0`.** ⇒ **The protocol module silently assumes `timeout = 0` and never says so.** Its `TbState` has no expired state, so the interleaving was not merely unchecked — **it was unrepresentable.** A model that cannot express a failure reports no violation, and that is indistinguishable from a model that ruled it out. **The spec was green for thirteen days on a case it could not see.** ⚠️ **And it makes exit criterion 3 load-bearing rather than tidy.** SPIKE-002 owes &quot;orphan detection and resolution, **including its time bound**&quot;. The time bound is not documentation — it decides which of two mutually exclusive designs is in force, and ADR-0015 and the formal spec currently hold opposite ones.">
          <outline text="blocks ADR-0015"/>
          <outline text="blocks SPIKE-002"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-023 — Four artifacts enumerate the rules surface SPIKE-005 must cover, and they disagree. `adr/ADR-0007:4…" _note="⭐ **AN ASSET'S CONVENTION IS NOT A PROPERTY OF THE ASSET, and that reaches the schema.** The 40% test is computed over everything placed in service in the ENTIRE tax year, so it cannot be resolved when an asset is acquired — only once the year closes. **A single late-December purchase retroactively changes the convention, and therefore the first-year deduction, of every personal-property asset placed in service earlier that same year.** Pub 946 works exactly that case with the dollars stated (L2628-2663): a machine placed in **January** takes the mid-quarter convention because of a computer bought in October. ⇒ **An engine that resolves convention at acquisition is wrong, and wrong SILENTLY** — every figure it produces is plausible. A per-asset schedule modelled as a pure function of the asset is structurally wrong whichever engine computes it. ⚠️ **The test's EXCLUSIONS are load-bearing too.** Real property is excluded from the numerator *and* the denominator, so a ratio computed over &quot;everything placed in service&quot; is wrong for any taxpayer holding buildings — and CFS holds `1600 Buildings` and `1603 Building Improvements`. ⚠️ **A SECOND gap, named by none of the four artifacts: listed property and §280F.** CFS holds `1700 Vehicles`; passenger automobiles carry §280F caps and a drop in business-use percentage triggers recapture (Pub 946 L3867 works it, at $4,018 of excess depreciation). Vehicles over 6,000 lb GVWR are treated differently, which for a rental house's box trucks is the ordinary case. **ADR-0030 already moved vehicle cost into COGS**, so this is not a hypothetical corner. ⇒ **The pattern is the enumeration being SHORT, not wrong** — the same shape as erp-spec#41, where SPIKE-011 was scoped narrower than the decision depending on it. **A scope list is a coverage claim, and nothing was checking this one.** Now something does: the coverage arm of `deno task dep-corpus` asserts every named facet is exercised, and it is currently RED.">
          <outline text="blocks SPIKE-005"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="HOT-024 — Two live, mutable statements about whether CFS still collects in Paxton contradict each other. **Th…" _note="⭐ **It is the case that decides what a permitted override target IS**, and it is why `ADR-0045` withdrew a decision that would have keyed the target set on live registrations. That rule would have made `paxton` newly assertable again — reversing a deliberate closure — while deleting `no_nexus`, which is deliberately offered and is **not** a registration at all. **Both non-trivial members come out wrong**, and the reason is that &quot;registered&quot; and &quot;assertable&quot; are different predicates that happen to coincide on the easy members. ⚠️ **And the ambiguity is not free while it stands.** `deriveJurisdiction`'s city table omits Paxton, so no address derives there; only a stored value reaches it. But `findTaxFor` will still price a `paxton` line at 6.25% today, because the window is open. ⇒ **the catalog would collect a tax the UI says CFS no longer collects**, and nothing fails. ⭐ **The cheap resolution is the one the convention already names**: if CFS is deregistered, write the 0% successor and the two statements agree. If CFS is still registered, the UI is wrong. **Either way the fix is one write, and the value of the hotspot is that nobody has decided which.**"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Decisions (25 in force, 19 proposed)">
        <outline text="In force (25)">
          <outline text="ADR-0001 — Replace Xero with an in-house ledger" _note="**In the context of** owning the order-to-invoice path but renting the ledger, **facing** a tracking-dimension defect that Xero silently absorbs and a costing model Xero cannot express, **we decided** to build the general ledger in-house, **to achieve** dimensional postings that cannot be null and a labour-absorption model we control, **accepting** that a large amount of unglamorous accounting surface area becomes ours to build and keep correct."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0002 — Bank feed sourced from Plaid, single Chase account" _note="**In the context of** needing bank transactions now that Xero's feed goes away, **facing** one bank and one account, **we decided** to source the feed from Plaid, **to achieve** a working feed without a bank-specific integration, **accepting** that Plaid's transaction model is a consumer-fintech model, not an accounting bank feed."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0003 — MongoDB for documents and masterfiles; TigerBeetle for the ledger" _note="**In the context of** replacing Firestore, **facing** two genuinely different storage problems — nested mutable business documents and an immutable balance-bearing ledger — **we decided** to use MongoDB for documents and TigerBeetle for the ledger, **to achieve** a ledger with enforced double-entry and real throughput alongside a document store that fits the order shape, **accepting** a two-store commit problem that has to be specified formally."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0004 — Retain Deno/Hono and TypeScript" _note="**In the context of** a greenfield rebuild that invites a stack change, **facing** a working Deno/Hono API and a one-person team, **we decided** to keep Deno, Hono and TypeScript, **to achieve** continuity of tooling and shared types with the clients, **accepting** that the TigerBeetle client may not load cleanly under Deno."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0005 — Retain SolidJS clients" _note="**In the context of** rebuilding the API underneath the operator UI, **facing** a working SolidJS manager app, **we decided** to keep SolidJS, **to achieve** reuse of the existing client and its component work, **accepting** that the reactive data layer must be rebuilt regardless because Firestore's real-time listeners go away."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0007 — Replace asset.accountant; dual GAAP/tax basis in scope" _note="**In the context of** building our own ledger, **facing** a separate hosted tool holding the asset register, **we decided** to bring fixed assets in-house with both GAAP and tax basis, **to achieve** depreciation postings that originate in the same ledger as everything else and a reportable deferred difference, **accepting** a genuinely intricate tax-depreciation rules problem."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0009 — Anticorruption layer — foreign identifiers never enter domain models" _note="**In the context of** integrating Plaid and any surviving external system, **facing** silent translation failures at a foreign boundary, **we decided** that foreign identifiers never enter domain models and an unresolvable id is a hard error, **to achieve** failures that are loud at the boundary instead of silent in the data, **accepting** that ingestion will refuse work that the current system would have accepted."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0010 — Accounting date vs posting timestamp policy" _note="**In the context of** a ledger whose store assigns its own timestamps, **facing** reporting that is periodised by business date, **we decided** to carry accounting date and posting timestamp as distinct fields with distinct rules, **to achieve** periodisation that survives late and back-dated entries, **accepting** that the ledger's native ordering is not the reporting ordering."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0011 — Fulfillment legs are first-class recorded events" _note="**In the context of** wanting COGS labour allocation, **facing** a current system that derives legs from order flags, **we decided** to record legs as first-class events, **to achieve** an actor, a clock and a shift on every movement, **accepting** that legs become data to be captured in the field rather than inferred for free."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0012 — Valkey-backed queues with in-process workers, replacing Cloud Tasks" _note="**In the context of** leaving Cloud Tasks along with the rest of the GCP platform, **facing** 16 queues whose config largely encodes HTTP-delivery hazards rather than domain requirements, **we decided** to run queues on Valkey with in-process workers and serialize on a per-entity lock, **to achieve** the removal of the duplicate-dispatch lease and its coupled-knobs invariant, **accepting** that durability becomes a configuration decision and worker liveness becomes ours to monitor."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0013 — Self-host on Linode, with Caddy fronting TLS" _note="**In the context of** rebuilding on a stack whose stores are all self-hosted anyway, **facing** a managed-service bill and a set of GCP primitives with no portable equivalent, **we decided** to host on Linode with Caddy terminating TLS, **to achieve** predictable cost and no platform lock-in, **accepting** that eight GCP managed services become ours to replace and operate."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0014 — Lifecycle state is derived from the ledger, never assigned" _note="**In the context of** deciding where order and invoice status live, **facing** a v1 corpus where stored status drifts from the facts that determine it, **we decided** that lifecycle state is derived from the ledger and materialized into MongoDB as a rebuildable projection, **to achieve** a stale status that is unrepresentable rather than merely monitored, **accepting** that a transition with no ledger consequence still needs a recorded fact."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0016 — Quint replaces TLA+ for the formal specs" _note="**In the context of** two formal specs that exist as never-executed stubs, **facing** a choice between TLA+ and Quint before either is written for real, **we decided** to use Quint, **to achieve** specs a one-person team will actually maintain and traces that can be replayed against the implementation, **accepting** a smaller ecosystem than TLA+'s."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0017 — Reporting authority is split by period state" _note="**In the context of** a ledger whose timestamps are posting time and reporting that is periodised by accounting date, **facing** a requirement that reported figures must not drift, **we decided** to split reporting authority by period state, **to achieve** closed-period figures that cannot drift at all, **accepting** that open-period figures come from the document store rather than the ledger."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0019 — Labor costing is NORMAL costing — wages actual per person, burden apportioned per run — and absorption measures utilisation together with rate variance" _note="**In the context of** bringing crew cost into COGS against the job that caused it, **facing** an employer of record that itemises wages per person and per day but prices employer burden per payroll RUN, **we decided** to cost labor as **normal costing** — wages actual, burden apportioned — **to achieve** a per-shift cost that reconciles to the invoice CFS actually pays, **accepting** a rate variance that is systematic rather than random and owes a period-close true-up. ⚠️ **This summary read &quot;we decided to cost labor at actual and let absorption measure utilisation&quot; until 2026-08-17**, when the Wrapbook exports measured otherwise (OQ-050, HOT-016). The title said the same and both were left behind by an amendment that corrected only the body."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0021 — Item type determines the revenue account; duplicate charge products are canonicalized" _note="**In the context of** duplicate active charge products booking the same economic event to different revenue accounts, **facing** posting rules that must map a billed line to exactly one account, **we decided** that the item type determines the account and the correctly-typed duplicate is canonical, **to achieve** a rule a posting engine can enforce, **accepting** that some historical revenue moves account under ADR-0020's restatement."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0022 — Invoice status decomposes into two derived projections" _note="**In the context of** an invoice status field that carries two different kinds of fact at once, **facing** two live defect populations that are one of each, **we decided** to decompose it into two independently derived projections, **to achieve** a state that cannot go stale and a voided invoice that cannot hold a balance, **accepting** that the single `status` enum every reader knows is retired."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0023 — Native Node-API addons load under Deno; the deployment unit is a self-extracting compiled binary" _note="**In the context of** a Deno API that must load three npm packages with native or native-adjacent dependencies, **facing** a `deno compile` deployment unit whose Node-API support was entirely unmeasured, **we decided** to ship a `deno compile --self-extracting` binary and forbid `--bundle`, **to achieve** a single deployable artifact on plain VMs, **accepting** a ~364 MB binary, a first-run extraction step, and a flag whose necessity nothing type-checks."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0024 — DuckDB is reached natively and server-side; client-side reporting is rejected" _note="**In the context of** sealed-period Parquet as the closed-period reporting authority (ADR-0017), **facing** a choice between the native DuckDB addon, WASM in the API process, and WASM in the browser, **we decided** to reach DuckDB natively and server-side and to reject client-side reporting, **to achieve** exact integer reporting over an artifact the server controls, **accepting** that reporting stays an API surface we have to build."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0026 — The general ledger is the GAAP book; the tax basis is a non-posting book derived at report time" _note="**In the context of** a fixed-asset register that must produce a full P&amp;L _and balance sheet_ on both a GAAP and a tax basis across years, **facing** a fleet where a §179 election expenses an asset in year 1 that GAAP carries for 5, 10 or 20, **we decided** that only the GAAP book posts to the general ledger and the tax book is derived at report time from the register's per-book schedules, **to achieve** two complete sets of statements without a second write path, **accepting** that the tax book has no double-entry enforcement of its own and is only as good as its derivation."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0030 — Vehicle cost moves from operating expense into COGS, absorbed and unabsorbed" _note="**In the context of** delivery being the largest tracked product line and carrying almost no cost, **facing** vehicle running costs sitting in operating expenses where no product line can see them, **we decided** to move vehicle cost into COGS on the same absorbed/unabsorbed shape ADR-0019 gave labor, absorbing on the shift's own allocation rows at a rate per computed mile, **to achieve** a delivery cost that is complete enough to allocate, **accepting** a second utilisation measure to maintain and a rate variance that labor does not have."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0034 — An accepted ADR is a historical record of the decision as taken; corrections live outside it and superseding is reserved for re-deciding" _note="**In the context of** two accepted ADRs whose evidence was later corrected, **facing** a lifecycle rule that forbids editing them and a supersede mechanism that would mean re-deciding things nobody disputes, **we decided** that an accepted ADR is a historical record of the decision as taken, **to achieve** an `adr/` directory that can be trusted to say what was actually believed and when, **accepting** that a reader of an ADR cannot tell from the ADR alone that one of its claims has since been retracted."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0036 — The ledger carries keys, not classifications — product line is derived at report time" _note="**In the context of** ADR-0029 having made allocation a reporting act, **facing** a chart that also carried the reporting _classification_ on the posting, **we decided** that a posting records keys — causal order(s), invoice, line — and never a product line, **to achieve** a ledger whose facts do not move when a category does, **accepting** that no dimensional balance can be read from the ledger without joining to the product master."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0037 — An id carries meaning where it is used — a headline on every id, and addressable claims inside a decision" _note="**In the context of** a spec whose authority is prose, **facing** a measured 22:1 mismatch between what is cited (a document) and what is claimed (about twenty statements inside it), **we decided** that an ADR additionally asserts individually addressable claims, typed as decision or premise, **to achieve** corrections that can be swept and premises that can go stale without touching a frozen decision, **accepting** a second surface on every ADR and a convention that only pays off as it is adopted."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0038 — Labor with no causal order is not COGS — 5801 is not created and 6600 Wages narrows instead" _note="**In the context of** but-for attribution putting a guaranteed day onto the order that caused the hire, **facing** the consequence that labor no order caused has no revenue it is applicable to, **we decided** that such labor is operating expense and stays in `6600 - Wages` rather than reaching a COGS account, **to achieve** a cost-of-revenue line that contains only costs of revenue, **accepting** that ADR-0019's `5801` is not created and that a superficially identical vehicle account deliberately does not follow."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Proposed (19)">
          <outline text="ADR-0015 — Inventory reservations are TigerBeetle pending transfers, over the operational window only" _note="review by: 2026-11-01 · **In the context of** wanting order state derivable and oversell prevented in the database, **facing** a balance model with no time dimension and an availability question that is entirely about time, **we decided** to model reservations as two-phase transfers over the operational window only, **to achieve** custody oversell that is unrepresentable and a fulfillment state that is derived, **accepting** that future-dated booking conflicts stay with the interval engine.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-022"/>
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-002"/>
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-012"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0020 — Xero history is recast, and the product line is derived from the master rather than assigned to the line" _note="review by: 2026-10-15 · **In the context of** migrating invoice history whose product-line classification lives in a line denorm the migration drops, **facing** a product master that already carries the assignment and a residue of custom lines with no master to carry it, **we decided** to derive the product line from the master and to record an explicit null where there is nothing to derive from, **to achieve** a recast history that invents no classification and whose gap is one countable row, **accepting** that v2's tracking-sliced view of closed periods will differ from Xero's by $231,796.26.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-006"/>
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-020"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-012"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-025"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-034"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0025 — A receipt that is not a categorised sale moves to 4800 Other Income rather than taking a null on an operating revenue account" _note="review by: 2026-10-01 · **In the context of** a chart holding receipts that are not sales at all — interest, cashback, vendor refunds — alongside genuine sales that map to no tracked product line, **facing** a reporting taxonomy that ADR-0036 removed from postings entirely, **we decided** that the difference between the two is carried by the ACCOUNT rather than by a value on the posting, **to achieve** a P&amp;L where a non-operating receipt is visible on the face of the statement rather than inside a dimension nobody reads, **accepting** that revenue mix changes shape when the history is recoded.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-011"/>
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-019"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-021"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-022"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-025"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0027 — Retain Mapbox for geocoding and Resend for transactional email, at the boundary" _note="review by: 2026-10-01 · **In the context of** a rebuild that replaces the ledger, the datastore and the host, **facing** two external services that already work and are not the reason for the rebuild, **we decided** to retain Mapbox for geocoding and Resend for transactional email as boundary services with no domain footprint, **to achieve** a smaller cutover surface, **accepting** two live third-party dependencies that every test must be prevented from reaching."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0028 — The self-hosted service tier — Gotenberg for rendering, the Victoria stack for observability" _note="review by: 2026-10-01 · **In the context of** ADR-0013 moving the system onto a host CFS operates, **facing** two service groups already running as containers, **we decided** to retain Gotenberg for document rendering and the full VictoriaMetrics stack for observability on that same host, **to achieve** one deployment unit and no new vendors, **accepting** that CFS is on the hook when either is down and that both fail quiet rather than loud."/>
          <outline text="ADR-0029 — The ledger records un-allocated facts; allocation is a specified reporting act" _note="review by: 2026-10-01 · **In the context of** labor and vehicle costs arriving in the ledger for the first time, **facing** a choice about whether a delivery's cost is posted to the goods it delivered or to delivery itself, **we decided** that the ledger records costs and revenues at the grain they occurred and never allocates, and that allocation happens once, in a specified report, **to achieve** a record that can answer questions nobody has asked yet, **accepting** that the un-allocated view shows the largest tracked product line running at a structural loss and must never be read as a managed P&amp;L.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-021"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-006"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-018"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0031 — The official product-line P&amp;L allocates by goods revenue on the causal order, declared as a proxy" _note="review by: 2026-11-01 · **In the context of** ADR-0029 promising exactly one official allocation and not saying what it is, **facing** three candidate bases that reassign up to a third of the largest product line's revenue between them, **we decided** to allocate by goods revenue on the causal order and to record in the report that this is a proxy rather than a cost driver, **to achieve** one reproducible margin per product line, **accepting** that the basis is the weakest tier in the standard criterion and stays that way until the shipping specs are populated.">
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-006"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-018"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-031"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-032"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-033"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0032 — The organization tree is a liability tree; projects and settlement points are addressing beneath it" _note="review by: 2026-11-15 · **In the context of** a customer master where 31 of 286 organizations are department clones and `Netflix Productions, LLC` is ten flat records with no parent, **facing** a requirement that invoices settle per department while credit exposure is a question about a company, **we decided** that the **organization** node is the legal entity and that projects and settlement points are addressing beneath it, **to achieve** a receivable that can be read both per department and per liable party, **accepting** that a separately-incorporated production is a separate organization and that the tree therefore cannot answer economic-concentration questions on its own.">
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-035"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-036"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-038"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-039"/>
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-006"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0033 — A document is addressed to exactly one node by a level-tagged reference, and unallocated credit sits at the settlement point" _note="review by: 2026-11-15 · **In the context of** ADR-0032's three-level organization tree, **facing** a receivable that must be readable per department and per liable party at once, **we decided** that every order and invoice is addressed to exactly one node through a reference that records which level it names, and that unallocated credit sits at the settlement point with cross-node application recorded as an explicit event, **to achieve** an AR partition that holds structurally rather than by computation, **accepting** that a single delivery serving two departments is two documents until someone asks for otherwise.">
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-030"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-038"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-040"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0039 — Historical ledger entries load as ordinary postings with cluster-assigned timestamps; the TigerBeetle `imported` flag is refused" _note="review by: 2026-11-15 · **In the context of** ADR-0020 restating Xero history into a ledger CFS owns, **facing** two TigerBeetle routes for loading dated history, **we decided** to load historical entries as ordinary postings with cluster-assigned timestamps and the accounting date in `user_data_32`, refusing `TransferFlags.imported`, **to achieve** a posting timestamp that survives the load and history that stays correctable after go-live, **accepting** that TigerBeetle's own timestamp range filter cannot reach historical periods and the projection must serve them.">
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-003"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0040 — The Zod schema is the sole authority and the MongoDB validator is generated from it one-way, with every rule the validator cannot carry named in a registry CI walks" _note="review by: 2026-11-15 · **In the context of** ADR-0003 moving documents into MongoDB, **facing** a `$jsonSchema` dialect that accepts 29 of 63 probed keywords and a Zod translator that discards constraints silently, **we decided** that the Zod schema is the sole authority and the MongoDB validator is generated from it one-way as a deliberately narrower artifact — `$jsonSchema` for shape, `$expr` for the cross-element items invariants — with every rule the validator cannot carry named in a registry CI walks, **to achieve** no rule enforced in neither layer, **accepting** that the generator must be written and maintained rather than taken off the shelf.">
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-006"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0041 — The labor rate variance posts as its own fact keyed to causal orders, and the plan burden rate is re-derived from each observed run" _note="review by: 2026-11-01 · **In the context of** an employer of record that itemises wages per person per day but prices employer burden per payroll RUN, **facing** an accrual made at a plan rate that is systematically wrong rather than merely imprecise, **we decided** to post the resulting difference once, as its own fact keyed to the causal orders, and to re-derive the plan rate from each observed run, **to achieve** a shrinking variance rather than a well-bookkept one, **accepting** that a job's margin is provisional until the run that paid for it is billed.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-010"/>
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-016"/>
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-018"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-045"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-050"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0042 — An orphaned pending transfer is resolved only by the application sweeper, which needs an intent record written before the reserve" _note="review by: 2026-11-15 · **In the context of** a two-store commit whose pending transfer can be orphaned by a crash, **facing** a ledger that expires such transfers blindly and cannot enumerate them at all, **we decided** that the application sweeper is the sole resolver and that an intent record is written before the reserve, **to achieve** an orphan that is always findable and always resolved against both stores, **accepting** that &quot;not held in limbo indefinitely&quot; becomes our SLO to meet rather than the database's to enforce.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-022"/>
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-002"/>
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-001"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0043 — The depreciation engine is hand-rolled behind a package boundary, computed per taxpayer-year, with the year's rules as versioned data" _note="review by: 2026-11-15 · **In the context of** an accepted decision to bring fixed assets in-house on both a GAAP and a tax basis, **facing** a registry with no US tax depreciation engine in it, **we decided** to build one behind a package boundary, computed per taxpayer-year against year-versioned rule data, **to achieve** an engine graded by a corpus rather than trusted, and an annual tax-law update that is a data change, **accepting** that correctness on filing-consequential numbers is now ours to own.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-023"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-054"/>
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-005"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0044 — CFS is the principal on a production service agreement, so the client's budget is revenue and the crew cost is CFS's cost" _note="review by: 2026-11-30 · **In the context of** a production service agreement whose accounting the charter asserts and the books contradict, **facing** an ASC 606 control test that turns on facts held by nobody in the data, **we decided** that CFS is the principal and reports the client's budget as revenue, **to achieve** one treatment where the live corpus currently carries two, **accepting** that the top line and every margin computed from it change, and that the history remains internally inconsistent.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-017"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-024"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-053"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0045 — A stored jurisdiction records WHO asserted it and under what authority — not which rung of the precedence answered, which is a restatement of fields the document already holds" _note="review by: 2026-11-30 · **In the context of** a jurisdiction model whose derivation is sound and whose stored record cannot say who asserted a value, **facing** a customer tree in which the rung a default lives at is not a rung anyone can attest at, **we decided** to record the asserter and their authority rather than the rung, **to achieve** a stored jurisdiction that can be told apart from CFS's own default, **accepting** that this is a new artifact rather than a field, and that three decisions an earlier revision of this ADR made are withdrawn as wrong.">
            <outline text="blocked on HOT-024"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-056"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-057"/>
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-008"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0046 — Adopt date-fns and @date-fns/tz as planned dependencies for every business datetime" _note="review by: 2026-11-01 · **In the context of** a rebuild whose rental days are Chicago days and whose stored business datetimes are already canonicalized to Chicago offset form, **facing** a choice between carrying forward the library that expresses that rule today and adopting the runtime's now-native `Temporal`, **we decided** to adopt `date-fns` and `@date-fns/tz` as planned dependencies for every business-datetime computation, **to achieve** continuity with the hard-won rule and one vocabulary shared by server and client, **accepting** that we are taking a dependency where the platform now offers a type that would make the defect class unrepresentable instead.">
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-008"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0047 — The client data model — one live transport replacing Firestore listeners, and an offline queue reconciled by three-way merge" _note="review by: 2026-11-15 · **In the context of** ADR-0003 replacing Firestore with MongoDB and ADR-0005 keeping SolidJS, **facing** a client whose live reads and whose write recovery are both defined in terms of a listener that will not exist, **we decided** one shared change-stream transport with a client-persisted resume token, plus an offline queue reconciled by three-way merge against a persisted base and surfaced as per-document notifications, **to achieve** a client that keeps working out of signal without silently discarding an operator's work, **accepting** a service worker's cache-invalidation problem, an unbounded divergence window, and an authored/derived partition that has to be built before the merge can be correct.">
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-009"/>
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-013"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-043"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-061"/>
          </outline>
          <outline text="ADR-0048 — The Plaid ingestion boundary — a store of record at the edge, CFS-minted identity, and delta-atomic application" _note="review by: 2026-11-30 · **In the context of** ADR-0002 sourcing the bank feed from Plaid and ADR-0009 fencing foreign identifiers out of domain models, **facing** a feed that mints new ids on every re-link, deletes rows without saying what they were, announces changes that are not changes, and quotes money as an unscaled float, **we decided** that the boundary holds a verbatim store of record, mints CFS identity itself, reconstructs correspondence by matching rather than by id, and applies a whole delta atomically, **to achieve** a feed whose failures are loud at the edge instead of silent in the reconciliation, **accepting** that the boundary store is a second copy of the bank's data that we are now obliged to keep.">
            <outline text="blocked on SPIKE-004"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-062"/>
            <outline text="blocked on OQ-063"/>
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Spikes (2 open)">
        <outline text="SPIKE-001 — Does the TigerBeetle client load and run under Deno via node-api compatibility?" _note="timebox: 2 days · closes: ADR-0023 · closed">
          <outline text="A Deno process creates accounts and posts a two-phase transfer against a real TigerBeetle cluster."/>
          <outline text="The same code path runs under `deno test` and under `deno compile`."/>
          <outline text="If it fails, the failure mode is characterised precisely enough to size the Go sidecar."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-002 — What commit protocol keeps a MongoDB document write and a TigerBeetle posting consistent across cra…" _note="timebox: 1 week · closes: ADR-0042 · closed">
          <outline text="Quint model checks clean for the three failure questions: can a pending transfer be orphaned; can a Mongo doc exist with no posted transfer; can a retry double-post."/>
          <outline text="A crash-injection harness reproduces each interleaving and the recovery path restores consistency."/>
          <outline text="Orphan detection and resolution is specified, including its time bound."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-003 — How does TigerBeetle's timestamp behave when loading history, and what are the `imported` flag's ti…" _note="timebox: 3 days · closes: ADR-0039 · closed">
          <outline text="Documented rule for which field carries accounting date and which carries posting timestamp."/>
          <outline text="A demonstrated history load whose accounting dates precede their posting timestamps by years."/>
          <outline text="Confirmation that live posting resumes correctly after an import batch."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-004 — What does Plaid actually provide, and what does bank reconciliation actually need?" _note="timebox: 3 days · closes: ADR-0048 · closed">
          <outline text="Pending vs posted semantics documented, including whether a pending transaction's id survives posting."/>
          <outline text="Backfill window established — how far back history reaches on first link."/>
          <outline text="Transaction id stability confirmed or refuted across updates and re-links."/>
          <outline text="Balance endpoints reconciled against a statement closing balance, to the cent."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-005 — Hand-roll the depreciation engine, or adopt a library?" _note="timebox: 1 week · closes: ADR-0043 · closed">
          <outline text="Test corpus covers: mid-month, half-year AND MID-QUARTER conventions; GDS vs ADS class lives; §179 and bonus effects on basis; §280F caps on listed property; partial disposals; prospective useful-life revisions; the deferred GAAP/tax difference. ⚠️ WIDENED 2026-08-22 (HOT-023) — mid-quarter is mandatory when the 40% test trips, not a third option, and §280F caps the combined §179 + bonus + MACRS figure this corpus computes."/>
          <outline text="Every candidate is scored against that corpus, with failures itemised rather than summarised."/>
          <outline text="A decision with a stated migration path if the library is later abandoned."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-006 — How lossy is translating JSON Schema 2020-12 to MongoDB's `$jsonSchema`, and what is enforced where…" _note="timebox: 2 days · closes: ADR-0040 · closed">
          <outline text="Itemised list of 2020-12 constructs unsupported by `$jsonSchema`, with the CFS schemas that use them."/>
          <outline text="A stated split — what the database enforces vs what the application enforces — with no construct unenforced in both."/>
          <outline text="A decision on whether the application schema or the Mongo validator is generated from the other."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-007 — Which access path reaches DuckDB from Deno — the native addon or WASM — and can a browser query a s…" _note="timebox: 2 days · closes: ADR-0024 · closed">
          <outline text="Both access paths working from Deno, with the mode matrix filled in for the native path."/>
          <outline text="A decision on the access path, with the memory ceiling of the WASM path stated as a number."/>
          <outline text="A verdict on client-side reporting via duckdb-wasm — viable or not, and at what corpus size it stops being viable."/>
          <outline text="An explicit fallback stated for the case where the native addon cannot be used, since no ADR names one today."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-008 — How do the Chicago Personal Property Lease Transaction Tax and Illinois home-rule sales tax apply a…" _note="timebox: 1 week · closes: ADR-0045 · closed">
          <outline text="Decision table covering every product type in the catalogue, including the mixed-regime case."/>
          <outline text="Reproduces the historical treatment on a sample of real invoices, with every disagreement explained rather than tolerated."/>
          <outline text="Nexus and rate-change handling specified, including how a rate change mid-rental is treated."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-009 — What replaces Firestore real-time listeners — MongoDB change streams plus a socket layer — and what…" _note="timebox: 1 week · closes: ADR-0047 · closed">
          <outline text="A working slice where a server-side document change updates a SolidJS view without a refetch."/>
          <outline text="Resume-token handling specified, including what happens after a disconnect longer than the oplog window."/>
          <outline text="An honest inventory of manager-side listener dependencies, with per-site effort — not a single aggregate estimate."/>
          <outline text="[object Object]"/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-010 — Does a production-grade Valkey queue client run under Deno, and can it express per-entity serializa…" _note="timebox: 3 days · closes: ADR-0012 · closed">
          <outline text="A Deno process enqueues, consumes, retries and inspects a failed job against a real Valkey."/>
          <outline text="Per-entity serialization demonstrated under concurrent workers, with a test that fails without it."/>
          <outline text="Repeatable jobs verified, including what is observable when the worker is down."/>
          <outline text="Whether per-key grouping needs a paid tier is answered with a citation, not a recollection."/>
          <outline text="If the client fails under Deno, the failure is characterised precisely enough to pick between another library and a sidecar."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-011 — Does TigerBeetle meet its durability and latency expectations on Linode block storage, or does it r…" _note="timebox: 3 days · closes: ADR-0013 · open">
          <outline text="Measured transfer throughput and latency percentiles on each storage option, as numbers, taken on a production-default cluster — NOT `--development`, which caps createTransfers at 253 against 8189."/>
          <outline text="A statement of whether block storage honours the durability assumptions TigerBeetle relies on, from the direct-I/O and fsync path verified explicitly rather than inferred from throughput looking fine."/>
          <outline text="The whole tier sized together as a table, one row per process — the nine Victoria-stack containers, the OpenTelemetry collector, Gotenberg, Caddy, the Deno application, MongoDB, Valkey and TigerBeetle — each carrying a memory and CPU figure that is measured or cited, never estimated."/>
          <outline text="The replica count and instance shape that sum implies, with monthly cost."/>
          <outline text="If block storage is unsuitable, the alternative is named and priced — and what that does to ADR-0013, which is accepted and frozen and can therefore no longer be gated by this spike."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-012 — At which fulfillment moment does a booking become a TigerBeetle pending transfer, and how much of o…" _note="timebox: 1 week · closes: ADR-0015 · in_progress">
          <outline text="A boundary chosen, with the transitions on each side enumerated."/>
          <outline text="Proof by replay that no future-dated booking consumes balance at the chosen boundary."/>
          <outline text="The derived/assigned split for order status stated field by field, as a table."/>
          <outline text="A count of orders in the replay whose status could NOT be derived, with the reason for each."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPIKE-013 — What does offline with queued writes actually cost, given that most fields save on focusout — how a…" _note="timebox: 1 week · closes: ADR-0047 · closed">
          <outline text="A queued field write survives a disconnect longer than the session and replays exactly once, with three offline edits to one field replaying as one write."/>
          <outline text="An offline create is addressable by subsequent queued writes before it reaches the server — so the identity decision (client-generated uid or otherwise) is made and demonstrated, not assumed."/>
          <outline text="A write that fails validation on replay lands somewhere a human sees it, with the field named; measured with the operator absent, which is the case that makes it hard."/>
          <outline text="The conflicts a same-field author/timestamp popover CANNOT arbitrate are enumerated from the live corpus with a count for each, not asserted as a list."/>
          <outline text="Whether the undo path requires OQ-043's document event history is answered yes or no, with what breaks if it is no."/>
          <outline text="The offline/pending/synced state is shown to be derivable at every save-on-focusout site — the absence of an error is currently the only feedback, so this is the load-bearing half."/>
          <outline text="[object Object]"/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Contexts (9)">
